meta-rzg3l-fast-boot
====================

Fast-boot layer for the RZ/G3L SMARC EVK. It shortens the time from reset to
the Weston desktop and keeps the BSP's Weston graphics stack unchanged.

  Phase 0   U-Boot environment: no boot delay, no environment reset, quiet
            kernel command line.
  Phase 1   Device-tree pruning and kernel configuration trimming.
  Phase 2   Rootfs slimming and systemd start-up optimisation.

The layer does not touch the display path. Combine it with
meta-rzg3l-seamless-logo-still to show a boot logo from U-Boot to Weston.

docs/fast-boot-method.md describes the measurement method and optimisation
patterns behind this layer, for reuse on other boards.

Tested with Yocto scarthgap, RZ/G3L BSP v1.0.0, u-boot 2024.07 and
linux-renesas 6.12.46-cip8, MACHINE = "smarc-rzg3l", core-image-weston.


I. What this layer changes
==========================

  U-Boot (recipes-bsp/u-boot/)
    fastboot.cfg sets CONFIG_BOOTDELAY=0. The patch removes "env default -a"
    from bootcmd and makes prodemmcbootargs a quiet command line without
    earlycon (section IV). prodsdbootargs keeps the BSP default.

  Kernel (recipes-kernel/linux/)
    The device-tree patch disables unused peripherals - Ethernet, USB, PCIe,
    CAN, camera, audio, extra UARTs, RTC - in rzg3l-smarc-fastboot.dtsi.
    fastboot.cfg removes the matching kernel subsystems, legacy PTYs and the
    wireless subsystem, and keeps the display, boot storage and thermal
    drivers.

  Distro (conf/distro/rz-vlp-fast.conf)
    Derives from rz-vlp. Pins linux-renesas 6.12, removes unused
    DISTRO_FEATURES / MACHINE_FEATURES and the development and benchmark
    package groups, disables the matching services and sets SERIAL_CONSOLES
    to ttySC3.

  Userspace
    fastboot-systemd-tweaks.bbclass masks vconsole setup, the unused ttyS0
    getty and systemd's network and identity services, and credits the saved
    random seed so that sysinit.target does not wait for the CRNG (see the
    comments in the bbclass for the first-boot caveat).
    systemd-conf_%.bbappend keeps the journal in RAM and stops forwarding it
    to the serial console.
    weston-init.bbappend lets Weston start in parallel with multi-user.target.


II. Required layers
===================

A standard RZ/G3L VLP project (RZ/G3L Linux Start-up Guide, chapter 2) plus
this layer:

  poky/meta
  poky/meta-poky
  poky/meta-yocto-bsp
  meta-openembedded/meta-oe
  meta-openembedded/meta-python
  meta-openembedded/meta-multimedia
  meta-arm/meta-arm-toolchain
  meta-arm/meta-arm
  meta-renesas/meta-rz-bsp
  meta-renesas/meta-rz-distro
  meta-rz-features/meta-rz-codecs
  meta-rz-features/meta-rz-graphics
  meta-rzg3l-fast-boot

The layer has priority 20 and must follow the Renesas BSP, distro and
graphics layers in bblayers.conf.

  Combining with meta-rzg3l-seamless-logo-still
  -----------------------------------
  The two layers are independent and can be used together or separately.
  Both patch r9a08g046l48-smarc.dts, in different places. Put
  meta-rzg3l-fast-boot first in bblayers.conf: both layers have
  priority 20, so their kernel patches are applied in bblayers.conf order,
  which is the tested order.


III. Build
==========

  $ source poky/oe-init-build-env build
  $ bitbake-layers add-layer ../meta-rzg3l-fast-boot

Set in conf/local.conf:

  MACHINE = "smarc-rzg3l"
  DISTRO  = "rz-vlp-fast"

Then:

  $ bitbake core-image-weston

Adding the layer applies phases 0 and 1 (bbappends). Phase 2 also requires
DISTRO = "rz-vlp-fast".

Metadata checks:

  $ bitbake-layers show-layers
  $ bitbake -e core-image-weston | \
        grep -E '^(DISTRO|PREFERRED_VERSION_linux-renesas|SERIAL_CONSOLES)='
  $ grep -E '^(bootdelay|bootcmd|prod.*bootargs)=' \
        tmp/deploy/images/smarc-rzg3l/u-boot-initial-env-smarc-rzg3l


IV. U-Boot environment
======================

Phase 0 is compiled into U-Boot. The default environment is:

  bootdelay=0
  bootcmd=run bootcmd_check;run bootimage
  prodemmcbootargs=setenv bootargs rw rootwait root=/dev/mmcblk0p2 quiet systemd.show_status=false

Compared with the BSP:

  bootdelay          2 -> 0. A key press on the serial console still stops
                     autoboot.
  bootcmd            "env default -a" removed, so a saved environment is no
                     longer reset at every boot.
  prodemmcbootargs   "earlycon" removed, "quiet systemd.show_status=false"
                     added. Kernel messages are still in the kernel log and
                     the journal, but not on the serial console. Add earlycon
                     back to debug failures that occur before the console is
                     registered.

U-Boot is part of fip, not of the rootfs. Write the new bl2 and fip to the
board (RZ/G3L Linux Start-up Guide); writing only the rootfs keeps the old
U-Boot.

  Boards with a saved environment
  -------------------------------
  The values above are defaults. They are used only while the environment
  area is empty or invalid. On a board where saveenv has been run, load the
  new defaults once after flashing:

    => env default -a
    => saveenv
    => reset

  Check with "env print bootdelay bootcmd prodemmcbootargs".

The build fails if any of the three values is missing from the generated
U-Boot environment.


V. Product constraints
======================

The device-tree profile disables Ethernet, USB, PCIe, CAN, camera, audio,
i2c0/i2c1/i2c3, extra UARTs and RTC. To use any of these, re-enable the
device-tree node and the matching kernel and userspace features.

i2c2 stays enabled (ADV7535 HDMI bridge). adc1 stays enabled (thermal
protection input). The microSD controller stays enabled for development; an
eMMC-only product can disable it once manufacturing and recovery requirements
are fixed.

systemd-resolved, -networkd, -timesyncd and -userdbd are masked. To add
networking, unmask them and re-enable the device-tree node and the
DISTRO_FEATURES entry.


VI. Measuring boot time
=======================

On the build host, confirm that every symbol disabled in fastboot.cfg is
really off - a "select" elsewhere can turn it back on:

  $ bitbake -c kernel_configcheck -f linux-renesas
  $ C=$(find tmp/work/*/linux-renesas/*/linux-*-build -name .config)
  $ grep -E '^(CONFIG_X=|# CONFIG_X is not set)' $C   # for each X in fastboot.cfg

On the target:

  # systemd-analyze
  # systemd-analyze blame
  # systemd-analyze critical-chain graphical.target
  # systemd-analyze plot > /boot.svg

  - Use graphical.target as the end point, not the serial "login:" prompt.
    serial-getty is Type=idle and prints later than the boot actually ends.
  - Measure ten boots and report the 95th percentile.
  - systemd-conf_%.bbappend sets MaxLevelStore=warning. Raise it to info for
    measurement boots that rely on info-level journal records.
